---
registryId: helm
---

{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is Helm?

**Helm** packages Kubernetes resource templates, default values, dependencies, and
metadata into a versioned **chart**. Installing or upgrading a chart creates a named
**release**: Helm's record of one rendered configuration and its revision history in a
Kubernetes namespace.

Helm matters when one application shape must be configured and promoted repeatedly.
Its core invariant is: **the chart and ordered values must render an inspectable set of
Kubernetes manifests before those manifests are allowed to change a cluster.** Helm is
not a workload controller, a database migration transaction, or a guarantee that the
rendered application behaves correctly.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Keep four boundaries separate

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="Chart" eyebrow="Package" icon="package" tone="blue" %}
Versioned templates, defaults, dependencies, schema, and metadata form the reusable
distribution unit.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Values" eyebrow="Input" icon="sliders" tone="violet" %}
Defaults, parent values, files, and command-line settings merge in precedence order
before rendering.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Manifest" eyebrow="Output" icon="file-code" tone="cyan" %}
Go templates produce ordinary Kubernetes YAML. Inspect this output rather than
reviewing templates alone.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Release" eyebrow="History" icon="history" tone="green" %}
A release tracks chart, values, namespace, and revisions. It does not own external
data changed by hooks.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}
{% /section-card %}

{% interactive-block id="technology/helm-complexity" dataFile="/api/content/technology/helm/data/values-precedence-model.json" /%}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Validate both input and rendered output

Use `values.schema.json` to reject missing, mistyped, or out-of-range input before it
reaches templates. Keep the public values surface small; every option becomes a contract
that chart maintainers must evolve.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/helm/code/values.schema.json" language="json" title="Constrain the chart's supported values" /%}

Then render the exact release inputs and validate the resulting resources:

- run `helm lint` for chart structure and conventions;
- run `helm template` with the production values and release context;
- apply schema, policy, deprecated-API, and security checks to the rendered YAML;
- use Kubernetes server-side dry-run where cluster API discovery matters;
- diff the proposed manifests from the current release before approval;
- keep chart and image artifacts immutable and record their digests.

Template success proves only that text rendered. It does not prove that an image exists,
a Service selects the intended Pods, a rollout reaches Ready, or a data change is safe.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Promote one chart version through environments

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Package immutable inputs" label="Build" %}
Pin dependencies and application artifacts; publish a new chart version instead of
mutating an existing package.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Render the target context" label="Verify" %}
Merge reviewed values, inspect manifests, and run policy and API validation against
the destination.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Upgrade with a recovery contract" label="Apply" %}
Choose wait, timeout, cleanup, and rollback behavior explicitly. Treat hooks as
separately owned programs.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Observe application evidence" label="Release" %}
Gate promotion on workload health and product signals, not only Helm command success.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/helm/code/verify-and-upgrade.sh" language="bash" title="Render, validate, and perform a bounded upgrade" /%}
{% /section-card %}

{% interactive-block id="technology/helm-upgrade-failure-lab" dataFile="/api/content/technology/helm/data/upgrade-failure-model.json" /%}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Design hooks and rollback for partial failure

- Hook Jobs block their lifecycle point until completion and can fail the release.
- Hook resources are not automatically managed like ordinary release resources; define
  deletion policy or TTL deliberately.
- `--wait` waits for supported resource readiness but does not automatically restore a
  failed revision.
- `--atomic` enables waiting and attempts to roll back release-managed changes after
  failure; verify the restored application afterward.
- A schema migration, message publication, or cloud API call performed by a hook may be
  irreversible from Helm's perspective. Prefer expand/contract data changes and
  idempotent jobs with their own recovery evidence.
- CustomResourceDefinitions have lifecycle constraints. Review CRD installation and
  schema evolution separately from normal namespaced resources.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="rose" %}
## Avoid complexity that templates cannot govern

- Split a chart when components have independent ownership or release cadence; do not
  create one switch for every possible topology.
- Prefer clear helper templates over deeply nested conditionals and dynamic name
  construction.
- Keep secrets out of values files and command histories; integrate a controlled secret
  delivery path.
- Test upgrades from every supported prior version, including failed hooks, readiness
  timeout, and rollback compatibility.
- Use a GitOps controller when continuous drift reconciliation and audited promotion
  are required. Helm can supply the package and renderer without becoming the controller.
{% /section-card %}

